Roundworms (C.
 elegans)
\series default
:
 These tiny worms share chemical danger signals.
 When one worm senses a threat,
 it releases a smell that warns nearby worms to run away or change their behavior.
 (*) 
\series bold
Sea Anemones
\series default
:
 Clonal anemones live in groups on rocks.
 They fight and sting "stranger" anemones to protect their group space,
 showing a basic form of group defense.
 (*) 
\series bold
Bacteria (Biofilms)
\series default
:
 While not animals,
 single–celled bacteria share nutrients and build protective slime shields together.
 Some even commit suicide to give food to their neighbors when times are hard.
